w23-scrutiny-not-authority
--------------------------

This directory is a clean-room Python 3 standard-library method sketch. It is
original work for this repository and contains no third-party code, data,
prompts, labels or weights.

Paper (idea credited)
---------------------
Eno Agolli.
"Can an AI Agent Be Epistemically Akratic? Higher-Order Evidence & the
Limits of Self-Correction". arXiv:2611.14889, v1, submitted 11 Nov 2026
(v1 Wed, 11 Nov 2026 01:27:29 UTC = 2026-11-11 10:27:29 KST). Primary
category cs.AI.
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14889
Paper licence: arXiv non-exclusive distribution licence,
http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/ (the paper is not under
CC BY; only the abstract is quoted or used).

Upstream code
-------------
None. The abs page links no code, and the paper is an argument with no
empirical code to run. Nothing was cloned or run.

Method only: the doctrine that a negative self-assessment can identify a
conclusion for further scrutiny without having authority to eliminate it,
and the abstract's remark that agreement between conclusions and
assessments can be reached by degrading either one. The case format, the
independence rule for checks, the four arms, the elimination gate, the
scoring, the oracle and the fixtures are this sketch's own. No text from
the paper is copied into the code; the PDF was not read; no model was
run. The paper's statements appear only in paper_evidence.json, quoted
verbatim from the abstract and marked UNVERIFIED.
